Tor and VPNs hide a user's IP address, and users often treat that as anonymity. We show that the text such users post remains a strong source of personal information, and that extracting it is now free. On #S.n_profiles synthetic authors from the SynthPAI dataset, two free-tier Gemini models inferred eight personal attributes from the authors' comments. The best model guessed sex correctly #pct(S.best_original.sex.acc) of the time, occupation #pct(S.best_original.occupation.acc) and country #pct(S.best_original.country.acc), with a mean of #pct(mean_best("best_original")) over all attributes; most of these attributes are never revealed by an IP address at all. A simple defense, rewriting comments with an LLM before posting, lowered mean accuracy to #pct(mean_best("best_rewritten")) but did not remove any attribute. We release all code and raw model outputs.
